
// Testing compiler directives and redefinability:

main :: int(){

}

// Thinking about making compiler directives essentially just be functions/macros
// that are defined identically to runtime ones, but are executed at compile time.
// And some can have special functionality in the compiler
#dosomething :: (){

}


// Would have to handle arguments differently though, right? since you should be able to
// have any AST node as an argument or return value
#return1 :: ast (x : ast){
    
}

// Or maybe make definition different. All arguments and return values are obviously
// going to be AST nodes, so why even specify that as the "type"?
#someDirective(A, B, C){
    
}

// Then we use the compiler directive alternatives to the builtin keywords. For example:
#someDirective(A, B, C){
    #if (A == B){
        return C;
    }
    #else{
        return #empty;
    }
}
// Once defined, you can use it like:
x : int = #someDirective(string(), "", 5);

// The main issue with the above definition is the fact that it might be difficult to 
// read what the directive is actually "returning". Even using `return` is bad I realize,
// so let me fix it:
#someDirective(A, B, C){
    #if (A == B){
        #return C;
    }
    #else{
        #return;
    }
}
// Now this is more clear. It returns the AST of C, or returns nothing. But there is
// another big problem with this. In what world do any compiler directives look like this?
// Sure, I could add extra syntax rules for directives, so that they can have 
// trailing scope body {} groups, like:
#something(){};
// And since () is optional with <= 1 arguments, it can become:
#something{};
// Also, scope braces are self-ending, so with them added the semicolon is no longer necessary:
#something{}
// But this adds more complexity for codegen and parsing. I'll have to think about it more
// One way it could be implemented right away is as regular directive arguments. Like:
#something(condtion, {scope});
// So I just pass the brace-enclosed scope body to the directive as an argument. Applied to
// the above example definition, it would become:
#someDirective(A, B, C){
    #if (A == B, {
        #return C;
    });
    #else({
        #return;
    });
}
// That is pretty good for now I would say. but definition is still weird. It is too close to 
// usage. Maybe I could just borrow the compile time define symbol? Like:
#someDirective(A, B, C) :: {
    // ...
}
// Or like:
#someDirective :: (A, B, C){
    // ...
}
// The main issue with defining like is is that the right hand side in both of these
// is not clearly a compiler directive. It would be parsed as a scope body in the first one,
// and as a broken function/lambda definition in the second one.
// Maybe you could use syntax like how `struct` and `module` are defined? Like:
#someDirective :: directive(A, B, C){
    // ...
}
// Although of course that is uausally used for return type in functions, and doesnt fix anything.
// So maybe keep arguments attached to the identifier:
#someDirective(A, B, C) :: directive{
    // ...
}
// But this still makes it seem like a normal expression, so I am not sure.
// Another option is to use another compiler directive to define a new one. Something like:
#directive(#someDirective, (A, B, C),
    {
        #if (A == B, {
            #return C;
        });
        #else({
            #return;
        });
    }
);
// The definition name could also be an issue. In the case above, how does the compiler 
// know if #someDirective is the name itself, or if it is a usage of another compiler directive which returns a name?
// Maybe the following would be a better way to do it. So the first argument must be a string, like:
#make_directive("someDirective", (A, B, C),
    {
        // ...
    }
);
// Since compiler directives with <= 1 argument can drop parens, you could do:
#make_directive #someDirective :: (A, B, C){
    // ...
};

// Although these are possibly needlessly complicated. For now, I will just write definitions
// by following the <name>(<args>) with curly braces, like:
#someDirective(A, B, C){}


// Now, continuing with the body of this directive, #if and #else are their own compiler
// directives, with no codegen context of each other.
// In other words, #else doesnt make any sense like this without some context.
// I could think about adding some context though. In fact, I could even define the
// #else and #else_if directives inside of ASA itself, as long as `#if` exists. Like:

// Define #else_if directive
#else_if(COND_EXPR, SCOPE_BODY){
    // Store the AST of the last used compiler directive into a variable called `prev`
    #var(prev, #stack_last("directives"));  // "directives" is a global stack, which is automatically kept up to date with the last used compiler directive. It is modified only after the directive fully runs
    // If the last used directive was a #if or #else_if
    #if(#nameof(prev) == "#if" || #nameof(prev) == "#else_if",
        // If it was, make sure it evaluated to false. If it didnt, then control
        // should not flow to this #else_if, and it should return nothing
        #if(#stack_pop("control_flow_result") == true, { #return; });

        // Then evaluate the condition. If true, return the scope passed as an argument
        #if(COND_EXPR, { #return SCOPE_BODY; })

        // Otherwise, it is false, or at least doesnt evaluate to something that could be called "true"
        // Push this result to the "control_flow_result" stack and return nothing
        #stack_push("control_flow_result", false);
        #return;
    );

    // If it wasnt a #if or #else_if, then this #else_if block is placed badly or there is a syntax
    // error somewhere.
    #usage_error("`#else_if` compiler directive must be placed after a `#if` or `#else_if` compiler directive");
}

// Define #else directive
#else(SCOPE_BODY){
    // Store the AST of the last used compiler directive into a variable called `prev`
    #var(prev, #stack_last("directives"));
    // If the last used directive was a #if or #else_if
    #if(#nameof(prev) == "#if" || #nameof(prev) == "#else_if",
        // If it was, make sure it evaluated to false. If it didnt, then control
        // should not flow to this #else, and it should return nothing
        #if(#stack_pop("control_flow_result") == true, { #return; });

        // Otherwise, it is false push true to the "control_flow_result" stack and return the scope body
        #stack_push("control_flow_result", true);
        #return SCOPE_BODY;
    );

    // If it wasnt a #if or #else_if, then this #else block is placed badly or there is a syntax
    // error somewhere.
    #usage_error("`#else` compiler directive must be placed after a `#if` or `#else_if` compiler directive");
}

// Using #make_directive() syntax:

// Define #else_if directive
#make_directive("else_if", (COND_EXPR, SCOPE_BODY), {
    // Store the AST of the last used compiler directive into a variable called `prev`
    #var(prev, #stack_last("directives"));  // "directives" is a global stack, which is automatically kept up to date with the last used compiler directive. It is modified only after the directive fully runs
    // If the last used directive was a #if or #else_if
    #if(#nameof(prev) == "#if" || #nameof(prev) == "#else_if",
        // If it was, make sure it evaluated to false. If it didnt, then control
        // should not flow to this #else_if, and it should return nothing
        #if(#stack_pop("control_flow_result") == true, { #return; });

        // Then evaluate the condition. If true, return the scope passed as an argument
        #if(COND_EXPR, { #return SCOPE_BODY; })

        // Otherwise, it is false, or at least doesnt evaluate to something that could be called "true"
        // Push this result to the "control_flow_result" stack and return nothing
        #stack_push("control_flow_result", false);
        #return;
    );

    // If it wasnt a #if or #else_if, then this #else_if block is placed badly or there is a syntax
    // error somewhere.
    #usage_error("`#else_if` compiler directive must be placed after a `#if` or `#else_if` compiler directive");
});


// Also, #usage_error might not be the best way to do the error. We want the error message
// to be where the `#else` and `#else_if` directives are actually used, rather than inside
// of its definition. Maybe another global stack? Like:
#make_directive("else_if", (COND_EXPR, SCOPE_BODY), {
    // ...
    #error("`#else_if` compiler directive must be placed after a `#if` or `#else_if` compiler directive", #current_directive);
});
// I am not sure if I would go with the naming of `#current_directive` since it is a bit
// unclear what that may mean. (is #current_directive the `#current_directive` node itself? 
// is it the `#error` node? is it the `#make_directive` node?)

// But I can make it so that the `#error` and `#warning` directives take an optional AST 
// argument that is the node the message should focus on. Otherwise, the message should 
// just be itself, showing the surrounding context.

// I could also make `#make_directive` pass another, implicit, argument. Like:
#make_directive("else_if", CONTEXT, (COND_EXPR, SCOPE_BODY), {
    // ...
    #error("`#else_if` compiler directive must be placed after a `#if` or `#else_if` compiler directive", CONTEXT);
});

// Or hide this under something like `#context`
#make_directive("else_if", (COND_EXPR, SCOPE_BODY), {
    // ...
    #error("`#else_if` compiler directive must be placed after a `#if` or `#else_if` compiler directive", #context);
});
// Ok that's probably the way to do it. #context, at least inside of the scope of a `#make_directive`,
// should refer to the AST node of where the directive is used. Or I could call it `#self` or `#this`, not sure.

// For now, I can implement the `#make_directive` compiler directive, even if a different
// way to define them is made in the future, the explicit syntax is useful to have
